      sources: "Sources: Chapter 01, “Dangers”; Learn, Known issues in Microsoft Graph (service principal members); project plan Gate 0.",
      say: `Every danger here has a guard in the code, and most of them are about something quietly going missing. The worst is an active-only inventory: as the project succeeds, the list shrinks, and the gate passes on an empty table. Close behind are a group counted as one person, a member dropped because its name couldn't be read, and an exceptions list nobody owns. Notice the one honest gap: service principals in a group aren't returned at all. And remember the inventory itself is sensitive, because it names every privileged holder.`,
      detail: `An active-only inventory: as holders become eligible, the list shrinks and Gate 0 passes on an empty table; guard: eligibility read and reported, and a test pins that the sample misses it. A group counted as one holder: the people behind PRV-T0-GlobalAdmin aren't reviewed or counted; guard: groups expanded, members marked Via Group, counted in PA-INV-04. A member dropped because its name wasn't readable: someone holds a role and isn't in the inventory; guard: rows kept with the ID, and PA-INV-09 says which permission is missing. Not guarded: a service principal member, which v1.0 doesn't return at all (see Verified facts). A custom role treated as harmless: a custom role with privileged permissions sits outside every tier; guard: Unclassified, PA-INV-05, never a default tier. An exception list nobody owns: standing privilege hides behind an "exception"; guard: an exception without an owner doesn't count (PA-INV-01). Reading only some Azure scopes: a management group or subscription you didn't list isn't read; guard: the manifest lists every request, so the reviewer sees the scopes read; discovering every scope is open (research gaps, row 4). Counting an Azure assignment twice: a schedule returned at two scopes inflates Owner counts; guard: de-duplicated by schedule ID, inherited rows skipped. The wrong cloud: a GCC High run reads graph.microsoft.com, or the token goes to another host; guard: explicit cloud, no default, host checked on every page, live sessions checked before the run. A broad permission: Directory.Read.All or a write permission granted "to make it work"; guard: the call list, and the test pins the task's exact permissions, none ReadWrite. The inventory as a leak: the folder names every privileged holder; guard: the manifest names no one, and Lab 2 deletes the output.`,
